        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="should-you-seal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Should You Seal Fireplace Brick at All?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most advice about sealing brick is written for the outside of a house, where the enemy is rain, freeze-thaw and the spalling that follows. An indoor fireplace surround faces none of that. It never gets rained on, it never freezes, and the brick on a living-room wall can go a century without a drop of water repellent and come to no harm at all. So the case for sealing it rests on different reasons, and it is worth being honest about whether those reasons apply to your fireplace before you spend a weekend on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first reason is soot. Every fireplace that burns wood, and plenty that burn gas, eventually leaves a grey-black shadow on the brick above the opening where smoke curls out when the fire is lit or the damper is slow to draw. On bare, porous brick that shadow sinks into the surface, and after a few seasons no amount of scrubbing quite brings the colour back. A sealer does not stop smoke from escaping, but it slows how deeply the residue soaks in, which turns a permanent stain into a cleaning job.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second reason is dust. Old, soft or tumbled brick sheds a fine red powder whenever it is touched or knocked, and the mortar joints shed grit of their own. A penetrating sealer binds the outer skin of the brick enough to stop most of that, which matters more than people expect on a hearth that gets swept, walked on and stacked with logs. The third reason is the hearth itself: ash, spilled drinks, candle wax and log-carrier mess land there, and an unsealed hearth brick holds onto all of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reasons not to seal are just as real. If the brick is painted or limewashed, there is nothing for a penetrating sealer to soak into. If the fireplace is in a historic house where the patina is the point, sealing changes the surface in ways that cannot be undone. And if smoke is pouring into the room, the fix is a chimney sweep and a draft problem solved, not a coating. For the outside of the stack, where weather really is the issue, our guide to the <a href="/best-chimney-sealer/">best chimney sealer</a> covers a different set of products and a different set of risks.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT ANSWER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Seal it if soot, dust or hearth stains bother you.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Indoor fireplace brick does not need protection from water the way exterior brick does. You are sealing for easier cleaning and a stable surface, so pick the product for the look and the cleanability you want, not for weather resistance.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="zones" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Where Sealer Goes and Where It Never Goes
          </h2>

          <p>
            A brick fireplace is really three surfaces built from different materials for different jobs, and the sealer question has a different answer for each. Treat the whole thing as one wall of brick and you will either waste product on surfaces that do not need it or, worse, put a coating where it should never be.
          </p>

          <p>
            The firebox is the chamber where the fire actually burns. In a masonry fireplace it is usually lined with firebrick laid in refractory mortar, materials made specifically to survive direct flame. It gets far hotter than anything a consumer brick sealer is designed for, and none of the sealer labels or data sheets we checked for this guide rate their product for use there. Leave the firebox bare. If the firebrick is cracked or the joints are open, that is a repair with refractory products made for the job, and a job for a chimney professional if the damage is more than cosmetic.
          </p>

          <p>
            The surround is the face of the fireplace: the brick around and above the opening, up to the mantel or the ceiling. This is where soot staining shows and where most people want the sealer to go. It gets warm when a fire is burning, especially in the first course or two around the opening, but nowhere near firebox temperatures. To give a sense of the margin involved, one fireplace-specific brick and stone sealer sold in the UK, Hotspot Brick and Stone Sealer, is rated by its maker as heat resistant to 90 degrees Celsius, about 194 degrees Fahrenheit, once dry. That is a sensible figure for a surround and nothing like the inside of a burning firebox.
          </p>

          <p>
            The hearth is the floor in front of the opening. It takes ash, embers that jump the screen, foot traffic and spills, and it is the surface that gains most from a sealer. The front edge of the hearth stays relatively cool; the strip right at the mouth of the firebox gets hot, so the same caution applies there as on the first courses of the surround. If your hearth is a stone slab rather than brick, the approach is closer to sealing a stone floor, and our guide on <a href="/how-to-seal-marble-floor-tile/">how to seal marble floor tile</a> covers the impregnating sealers that suit polished and honed stone.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Zone</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Seal it?</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Firebox interior</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Never</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Direct flame; firebrick and refractory mortar are made to stand bare</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Inner return of the opening</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Too close to the fire; stop at the outer face</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Surround face</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Soot and dust protection; warm but not hot</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hearth</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ash, spills and wear; keep off the strip at the firebox mouth</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Painted or limewashed brick</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually no</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Nothing for a penetrating sealer to enter</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The practical rule is to mask off the opening and stop the sealer at the outer face of the brick, leaving the inside return of the opening and everything behind it untreated. If you have a gas insert or a wood stove sitting in the old fireplace, read its manual too. Inserts and stoves come with their own clearance and surface requirements, and the manual is the document that governs anything close to the unit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="penetrating-vs-film" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Penetrating vs Film-Forming Sealers, and the Look Each Gives
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every brick sealer falls into one of two families, and the choice between them decides both what the fireplace will look like and how it will behave over the years. The general version of this argument is laid out in our comparison of <a href="/acrylic-vs-penetrating-concrete-sealer/">acrylic vs penetrating sealers</a> and our explainer on <a href="/impregnating-vs-topical-stone-sealer/">impregnating vs topical sealers</a>; this section is about what each one means on a fireplace specifically.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Penetrating sealers</h3>

          <p>
            Penetrating sealers, usually silane, siloxane or a blend of the two, soak into the pores of the brick and mortar and react there. They leave no layer on top, so the brick keeps its natural texture and, with most products, its natural colour. Water beads on the surface instead of soaking in, and that same repellency slows down how far soot and spills can travel into the brick. Because nothing sits on the face, there is nothing to peel, blister or turn cloudy, and the brick can still release moisture as vapour.
          </p>

          <p>
            Breathability matters indoors more than people assume. A chimney breast on an exterior wall can pick up moisture from the chimney above, from rain that gets past the crown, or from condensation, and that moisture needs a way out. Brick that is allowed to breathe dries harmlessly; brick that is sealed under a film can push salts to the surface behind the coating, the same efflorescence problem described in our guide to <a href="/efflorescence-on-basement-walls/">efflorescence on basement walls</a>. The limitation of a penetrating sealer is that it makes soot harder to set in rather than easy to wipe off. You will still clean the face; you will just get further with less effort.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Film-forming sealers</h3>

          <p>
            Film-formers, typically water-based acrylics, dry as a thin clear layer on the face of the brick. They are sold in satin and gloss sheens, and they darken and saturate the colour of the brick, sometimes a little and sometimes a lot. The upside is genuine wipeability: soot and spills sit on top of the film and come off with a damp cloth. The downsides are that the film changes the look of the fireplace for good, can trap moisture in brick that needs to breathe, and puts a layer of plastic on the surface that gets warmest when the fire is lit.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Penetrating (silane/siloxane)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Film-forming (acrylic)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Look</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unchanged, natural flat finish</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Darker colour, satin or gloss sheen</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Soot protection</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slows staining; still needs cleaning</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wipes off the film surface</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Breathability</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brick can still release vapour</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Restricted; can trap moisture</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Failure mode</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gradually loses repellency</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Can peel, cloud or scuff</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Best fit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Working wood-burning fireplaces, old brick</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Decorative or rarely used fireplaces</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For a fireplace that gets used through the winter, a penetrating sealer is the safer default: it changes nothing you can see, it cannot peel off the warm courses around the opening, and it never needs stripping. A film-former makes more sense on a decorative fireplace that is rarely or never lit, where you want the brick to look richer and you value wipe-clean over breathability. If you are unsure, the test patch described below will settle the look question in an afternoon.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Clean the Brick First, Properly
          </h2>

          <p>
            A sealer locks in whatever is on the brick at the moment it goes on. Seal over a soot shadow and the shadow becomes part of the finish, now protected from the very cleaning that might have removed it. This is the step that decides how the job looks, and it is worth more time than the application itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the fireplace completely cold: no fire for at least a day, the ashes cleared out, and the damper closed so you are not pulling cleaning dust up the flue or soot down it. Lay drop cloths over the floor and tape plastic over the firebox opening. Then vacuum the whole surround with a brush attachment to lift loose dust and ash from the brick texture and the mortar joints; a decent <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vac</a> with a fine filter is ideal, because a household vacuum will blow fine soot straight back into the room.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, take off the loose soot dry. A dry-cleaning soot sponge, the vulcanised rubber kind sold for fire restoration, lifts surface soot without smearing it, which is exactly what a wet cloth does on the first pass. Work the sponge in one direction and turn it as it loads up. Only then move to wet cleaning: warm water with a mild detergent or a cleaner sold specifically for removing soot from brick and stone, a nylon scrub brush, and a bucket of clean rinse water. Avoid wire brushes, which scar the brick face and leave metal fragments that rust, and keep the water to a minimum, because brick drinks it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leave acid cleaners out of it. Muriatic acid is a tool for removing mortar smears from new exterior brickwork; indoors it gives off fumes, it attacks mortar joints, and the run-off will damage almost any floor finish it reaches. If you see white powdery deposits rather than soot, that is efflorescence, a sign that moisture is moving through the brick. Brush it off dry, and find out where the water is coming from before you seal anything, because sealing over an active moisture problem only moves it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, let the brick dry thoroughly. After wet cleaning that usually means several days with the room heated and ventilated. A simple way to check is the plastic sheet test borrowed from concrete work: tape a square of clear plastic tightly to the brick, leave it for a day, and look for condensation underneath. Fog on the plastic or a darker patch of brick means it is not ready. Most penetrating sealers want dry brick to soak into, and a film-former applied over damp brick can cloud.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Apply Fireplace Brick Sealer, Step by Step
          </h2>

          <p>
            Read the label of the specific product before you open it. Application method, number of coats, coverage and drying time vary, and the label overrides any general guide, including this one. What follows is the sequence that works for most interior brick sealers, with the points where penetrating and film-forming products differ.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">1. Mask and protect</h3>

          <p>
            Tape off the mantel, any wood trim, the insert or glass doors, and the edge of the firebox opening so the sealer stops at the outer face of the brick. Cover the floor well beyond the hearth. Penetrating sealers in particular are thin and run easily, and a drip on a hardwood floor or stone tile can leave a mark that will not come out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">2. Do a test patch</h3>

          <p>
            Pick an inconspicuous spot, such as a low corner of the surround or the side of a hearth step, and apply the sealer exactly as you plan to do the whole fireplace. Let it dry fully, ideally overnight, then look at it in daylight and under the room lights. Penetrating sealers can still deepen the colour slightly on some brick, and film-formers can turn out glossier or darker than the sample on the can suggests. This is the only chance you get to change your mind.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">3. Apply in manageable sections</h3>

          <p>
            Indoors, a brush or a short-nap roller gives far more control than a sprayer, and it keeps mist out of the air you are breathing. With a penetrating sealer, the goal is to flood the brick so it absorbs evenly, working mortar joints as well as brick faces, and then to brush out any runs or puddles before they dry. Some products specify more than one coat applied wet on wet, meaning the second goes on while the first is still damp; Ghostshield, for example, specifies two coats wet on wet for its Siloxa-Tek 8500. With a film-former, the opposite applies: thin, even coats, with any excess brushed out of the joints and texture before it skins over, because heavy spots dry as shiny patches.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">4. Let it dry and cure</h3>

          <p>
            Drying and curing are not the same thing. A surface can be dry to the touch in a few hours while the sealer is still reacting inside the brick. For its Siloxa-Tek 8500, Ghostshield gives a typical drying time of four to six hours at 70 degrees Fahrenheit and 50 per cent relative humidity, and says water repellency keeps developing for up to seven days. Do not light a fire the same day you seal. Wait at least until the label says the product is fully dry, and longer if it gives a cure time; the Hotspot data sheet, for instance, recommends leaving its sealer to dry overnight before relying on its heat resistance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the result with a few drops of water on a sealed area once the product has cured. On a penetrating sealer the water should bead and sit on the surface rather than darkening the brick. If a patch drinks the water, it was missed or under-applied, and a touch-up there is easier now than after the next season of soot.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Safety: Fumes, Flammability and Pilot Lights
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealing a fireplace is one of the few masonry jobs most people do indoors, in a closed room, right next to an appliance designed to make fire. That combination deserves a few minutes of planning, and most of it comes down to the carrier the sealer uses.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solvent-based sealers carry their active ingredients in petroleum solvents that evaporate as the product dries, and those vapours are flammable. The fireplace must be completely cold, with no embers in the ash. If you have a gas fireplace or insert, turn the pilot light off and leave it off until the room has fully aired out, and think about other ignition sources too: a furnace or water heater in an adjacent space, candles, a space heater. The UK Hotspot sealer, a solvent-based product, is labelled flammable for exactly this reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-based sealers remove most of that worry and almost all of the smell, which is why they are the sensible default for indoor fireplace work. The difference can sit within a single brand: Ghostshield describes its Siloxa-Tek 8500 as a water-based formula and its Siloxa-Tek 8510 as solvent carried. Both list brick and indoor use, but for a sealer that will dry in your living room, the water-based version is the easier one to live with.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever the product, ventilate. Open windows, run a fan blowing out rather than in, and keep children and pets out of the room until the smell has gone. Wear chemical-resistant gloves and eye protection, because sealer splashes are easy to get into your eyes when you are working overhead on the upper courses. Read the safety data sheet if the label points you to one; it lists the solvent, the exposure limits and the right respirator cartridge, if one is needed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, deal with the rags properly. Some brick and stone sealers contain drying oils, and rags soaked in drying oils can heat up on their own as they cure and, in the worst case, catch fire in a pile. Lay used rags flat outdoors to dry, or put them in a sealed metal container filled with water, and dispose of them according to local rules. It is an unglamorous step, and it is the one most likely to matter.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">BEFORE YOU OPEN THE CAN</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Cold firebox, pilot off, windows open.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the product is solvent-based, treat the room like a workshop with flammable vapour in it until it has fully aired out. If it is water-based, the risk drops sharply, but ventilation and gloves still apply.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Choosing a Fireplace Brick Sealer
          </h2>

          <p>
            There is no such thing as a fireplace-only sealer category in most US stores; you are choosing a brick or masonry sealer that suits an interior surround. The questions that narrow it down are the ones this guide has already raised: what look you want, whether the fireplace is used, and whether you are happy with solvent fumes indoors.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a working fireplace where you want the brick to look exactly as it does now, a water-based silane/siloxane penetrating sealer is the default. Ghostshield Siloxa-Tek 8500 is a verified example: the maker describes it as a water-based silane/siloxane blend that lists brick and masonry among its surfaces, is rated for indoor and outdoor use, and dries invisible with a natural flat finish. That is precisely the profile a fireplace surround needs. Expect it to make soot easier to clean rather than to make it disappear.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a decorative fireplace where you want richer colour and wipe-clean brick, a water-based acrylic masonry sealer in a satin sheen is the usual choice, with gloss only if you genuinely want a shiny surround. Keep film-formers off the first courses around the opening on any fireplace that gets lit, and do the test patch, because the darkening effect varies a great deal between brick types. For a ranked list of the brick sealers that suit interior, exterior and chimney work, see our <a href="/best-brick-sealer-reviews/">best brick sealer reviews</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, maintenance is light indoors. A penetrating sealer on a surround that is never washed down can keep working for many years; use the water-drop test once a year and reseal only the patches that have stopped beading, usually the hearth and the courses right above the opening. A film-former will show wear first on the hearth, where it gets walked on; clean it with mild soap and water, never with abrasive pads, and plan on a maintenance coat when it starts to look dull or scuffed.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are also dealing with the outside of the chimney, treat it as a separate project with separate products. Exterior chimney brick needs a breathable water repellent and often crown or flashing repairs that no sealer can replace, which is why it has its own guide to the <a href="/best-chimney-sealer/">best chimney sealer</a> rather than a paragraph here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Over Soot</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sealer locks in whatever is on the brick. Seal over a grey smoke shadow and it becomes a permanent part of the finish, now protected from the cleaning that might have removed it. Vacuum, dry-sponge and wash the brick, then let it dry for several days before the first coat goes on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Inside the Firebox</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The firebox is lined with firebrick and refractory mortar made to stand bare against direct flame, and no consumer brick sealer we found is rated for that heat. Mask the opening, stop at the outer face of the brick, and leave the firebox and the inner return of the opening untreated.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Gloss Film on a Working Fireplace</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A gloss acrylic film darkens the brick, changes its character for good and puts a plastic layer right where the surround gets warmest. On a fireplace that is lit through the winter, a penetrating sealer avoids the peeling, clouding and stripping that a film can eventually need.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Test Patch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Even penetrating sealers can deepen the colour slightly on some brick, and film-formers can dry far darker or shinier than the can suggests. Seal a hidden corner, let it dry overnight, and look at it in daylight before committing the whole surround to a look you cannot undo.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Lighting a Fire Too Soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dry to the touch is not cured. Wait at least until the label says the product is fully dry, and longer if it gives a cure time. With solvent-based products, keep the pilot light off and the room ventilated until the smell has completely gone.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should you seal fireplace brick?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Seal it if soot staining, brick dust or hearth stains bother you. Interior fireplace brick does not need protection from rain the way exterior brick does, so sealing is about easier cleaning and a stable, non-dusty surface. Skip it on painted or limewashed brick, and on historic brick where the patina matters more than cleanability.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best sealer for fireplace brick?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a fireplace that gets used, a water-based penetrating silane/siloxane sealer is the safest default, because it leaves the brick looking unchanged, cannot peel off the warm courses and keeps fumes low indoors. For a decorative fireplace where you want richer colour and wipe-clean brick, a water-based acrylic in a satin sheen is the usual choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal the inside of a fireplace?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The firebox is lined with firebrick and refractory mortar that are made to stand bare against direct flame, and consumer brick sealers are not rated for those temperatures. Stop the sealer at the outer face of the brick, leave the inner return of the opening untreated, and use refractory products for any firebox repair.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will sealing brick stop soot stains?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It slows them down rather than stopping them. A penetrating sealer reduces how deeply soot soaks into the brick, so stains clean off more easily, while a film-former lets soot be wiped from the surface. Neither stops smoke escaping into the room; persistent smoke staining usually points to a draft or chimney problem worth having inspected.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sealing fireplace brick change its colour?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Penetrating sealers are designed to leave brick looking unchanged, though some brick deepens slightly. Film-forming acrylics darken and saturate the colour and add a satin or gloss sheen. Because the effect varies so much between brick types, always seal a hidden test patch and let it dry fully before deciding.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after sealing can you use the fireplace?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow the product label, and never light a fire the same day. Ghostshield, for example, gives four to six hours of drying time for its Siloxa-Tek 8500 at 70 degrees Fahrenheit, with repellency developing over a week. Waiting until the next day at minimum, with the room fully aired out, is a sensible floor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often do you need to reseal fireplace brick?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Far less often than exterior brick, because indoor brick sees no weather. Put a few drops of water on the hearth and the courses above the opening once a year; where water still beads, the sealer is working. Reseal only the patches that darken and absorb, which are usually the hearth and the area above the fire.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

