        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Sealing fireplace brick is a cleaning-and-appearance job, and it goes well when you respect one boundary: the surround and the hearth get sealer, the firebox never does. Clean the soot off first, let the brick dry, and test the product on a hidden corner before you commit.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For a fireplace you actually use, a water-based penetrating sealer is the low-risk choice that leaves the brick looking as it does today. Save the satin acrylic look for decorative fireplaces, keep the room ventilated and the pilot off while you work, and do not light a fire until the label says the sealer is ready.
          </p>
